1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,233
Welcome to chapter seven of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,233 --> 00:00:11,103
Part three begins, and it begins not with the model but with the data.

3
00:00:11,103 --> 00:00:16,877
Garbage in, garbage out is truer for language models than for almost anything else.

4
00:00:16,877 --> 00:00:22,721
Today we build a complete data pipeline: clean, deduplicate, split, chunk and store.

5
00:00:22,721 --> 00:00:33,157
I ran the book's chapter seven notebook, and I also pushed three real public domain novels through the same pipeline to see what happens on real text.

6
00:00:33,157 --> 00:00:37,470
And I found a couple of mistakes in the book's worked example.

7
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:42,413
In part two we learned Python, tensors and the training loop.

8
00:00:42,413 --> 00:00:46,036
Today we produce a clean dataset in JSON lines format.

9
00:00:46,036 --> 00:00:51,805
In chapter eight we tokenize it, and from there we embed it, model it and train on it.

10
00:00:51,805 --> 00:00:57,307
Everything follows the chapter seven notebook, which is linked in the description.

11
00:00:58,157 --> 00:00:59,457
Here's the roadmap.

12
00:00:59,457 --> 00:01:00,826
Why quality matters.

13
00:01:00,826 --> 00:01:03,564
Where to get text you're allowed to use.

14
00:01:03,564 --> 00:01:08,630
Five new Python tools: files, sets, JSON, regular expressions and hashing.

15
00:01:08,630 --> 00:01:16,501
Then the pipeline itself: cleaning, deduplication, splitting without leakage, chunking, storage and quality checks.

16
00:01:16,501 --> 00:01:20,471
Then my experiment with three real novels, and the review.

17
00:01:21,322 --> 00:01:22,350
The vocabulary.

18
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:25,641
A corpus is the collection of text you train on.

19
00:01:25,641 --> 00:01:29,550
Splits divide it into training, validation and test sets.

20
00:01:29,550 --> 00:01:36,339
Data leakage is when evaluation data sneaks into training, making scores look better than they are.

21
00:01:36,339 --> 00:01:38,807
Deduplication removes repeated text.

22
00:01:38,807 --> 00:01:42,579
A chunk is a piece of long text sized to fit the model.

23
00:01:42,579 --> 00:01:47,859
The context window is the maximum number of tokens the model can see at once.

24
00:01:47,859 --> 00:01:51,081
And JSON lines stores one JSON object per line.

25
00:01:51,932 --> 00:01:52,561
Part one.

26
00:01:52,561 --> 00:01:54,939
Why data quality rules everything.

27
00:01:55,790 --> 00:02:02,949
Language models happily memorise whatever you give them: typos, navigation menus, licence boilerplate, spam.

28
00:02:02,949 --> 00:02:06,860
Cleaning your data is the cheapest accuracy boost there is.

29
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:13,887
The book's analogy is cooking: fresh ingredients and a good recipe beat a fancy stove with rotten produce.

30
00:02:13,887 --> 00:02:19,588
The pipeline goes raw text, clean, deduplicate, split, chunk, and store as JSON lines.

31
00:02:19,588 --> 00:02:26,019
And because each stage inherits the problems of the one before, check the data after every stage.

32
00:02:26,869 --> 00:02:28,438
Where do you get text?

33
00:02:28,438 --> 00:02:32,433
You don't need billions of tokens to learn the pipeline.

34
00:02:32,433 --> 00:02:34,287
A few megabytes is plenty.

35
00:02:34,287 --> 00:02:42,205
Use public domain books like Project Gutenberg, permissively licensed text, documentation, or your own writing.

36
00:02:42,205 --> 00:02:50,408
Avoid personal data, sensitive records, and giant scraped dumps, where you inherit every mess and every legal risk.

37
00:02:50,408 --> 00:02:55,829
And keep a small README recording where each text came from and its licence.

38
00:02:55,829 --> 00:02:58,183
For my experiment I used three H.

39
00:02:58,183 --> 00:02:58,326
G.

40
00:02:58,326 --> 00:03:04,175
Wells novels from Project Gutenberg, which are public domain in the United States.

41
00:03:05,025 --> 00:03:05,706
Part two.

42
00:03:05,706 --> 00:03:07,372
Five new Python tools.

43
00:03:08,222 --> 00:03:09,575
Three of the tools.

44
00:03:09,575 --> 00:03:18,768
Files: open with a with block, which closes the file automatically even if something crashes, and always pass encoding utf eight.

45
00:03:18,768 --> 00:03:27,604
Sets: they store only unique items, and checking whether something is in a set is instant, while a list checks item by item.

46
00:03:27,604 --> 00:03:32,521
That matters when you ask have I seen this before thousands of times.

47
00:03:32,521 --> 00:03:43,210
JSON: dumps converts a dictionary to a string, and ensure ascii equals false keeps characters like Chinese or emoji readable instead of escaping them.

48
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:49,624
JSON lines puts one record per line, so you can stream a huge file without loading it all.

49
00:03:50,474 --> 00:03:53,096
Regular expressions describe patterns.

50
00:03:53,096 --> 00:04:00,343
Less than, then one or more characters that aren't greater than, then greater than, matches any HTML tag.

51
00:04:00,343 --> 00:04:03,587
Backslash s plus matches any run of whitespace.

52
00:04:03,587 --> 00:04:07,038
And hashing gives a short fingerprint of any text.

53
00:04:07,038 --> 00:04:13,525
The same input always gives the same hash, and a tiny change gives a completely different one.

54
00:04:13,525 --> 00:04:18,702
I checked the book's SHA-1 values for hello and world, and they're correct.

55
00:04:18,702 --> 00:04:25,604
For deduplication we compare forty character fingerprints instead of eight hundred character chunks.

56
00:04:26,454 --> 00:04:27,619
One correction.

57
00:04:27,619 --> 00:04:33,988
The book says SHA-1's collision risk is roughly one in two to the power one sixty.

58
00:04:33,988 --> 00:04:45,716
That's the size of the output space, but the birthday paradox means collisions become likely after about two to the power eighty hashes, not one sixty.

59
00:04:45,716 --> 00:04:52,784
And SHA-1 is cryptographically broken: researchers produced a deliberate collision in 2017.

60
00:04:52,784 --> 00:05:00,940
For removing accidental duplicates from a small corpus, the book's conclusion still holds: SHA-1 is fine.

61
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:05,445
But if anyone could craft inputs against you, use SHA-256.

62
00:05:06,295 --> 00:05:07,031
Part three.

63
00:05:07,031 --> 00:05:08,706
Cleaning and normalising.

64
00:05:09,556 --> 00:05:11,676
Here's the cleaning function.

65
00:05:11,676 --> 00:05:30,763
First, NFKC Unicode normalisation, which converts compatibility characters to standard forms: the fi ligature becomes two letters, full width digits become normal digits, and the ellipsis character becomes three dots, which you can see in the notebook's output.

66
00:05:30,763 --> 00:05:33,762
Accented letters like é stay as they are.

67
00:05:33,762 --> 00:05:40,929
Then strip HTML tags, turn tabs and carriage returns into spaces, and collapse runs of whitespace.

68
00:05:40,929 --> 00:05:43,269
Notice the smart quotes survive.

69
00:05:43,269 --> 00:05:45,389
NFKC doesn't straighten them.

70
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:47,748
A few policy decisions.

71
00:05:47,748 --> 00:05:54,764
Lowercasing shrinks the vocabulary but loses information, for example US the country versus us the pronoun.

72
00:05:54,764 --> 00:05:57,583
Either choice is fine if you're consistent.

73
00:05:57,583 --> 00:06:01,911
Keep punctuation, because models learn sentence structure from it.

74
00:06:01,911 --> 00:06:05,911
Keep emoji and non-Latin scripts only if you'll support them.

75
00:06:05,911 --> 00:06:12,074
And always look at the text before and after cleaning to make sure nothing important vanished.

76
00:06:12,924 --> 00:06:13,637
Part four.

77
00:06:13,637 --> 00:06:15,634
Deduplication and filtering.

78
00:06:16,483 --> 00:06:21,484
Models memorise duplicates instead of learning patterns, so remove them early.

79
00:06:21,484 --> 00:06:26,869
Hash every chunk, keep a set of hashes you've seen, and keep only first occurrences.

80
00:06:26,869 --> 00:06:29,561
continue skips to the next loop iteration.

81
00:06:29,561 --> 00:06:32,574
The notebook goes from four documents to three.

82
00:06:32,574 --> 00:06:42,190
Then filter: drop chunks that are very short, under forty characters, or very long, over two thousand, and optionally run a language or content check.

83
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:45,183
One limitation to keep in mind.

84
00:06:45,183 --> 00:06:47,809
Hashing only catches exact duplicates.

85
00:06:47,809 --> 00:06:53,822
The cat sat full stop and the cat sat exclamation mark get completely different hashes.

86
00:06:53,822 --> 00:07:01,839
Large scale pipelines use near duplicate detection, like MinHash with locality sensitive hashing, or n-gram overlap.

87
00:07:01,839 --> 00:07:09,095
A cheap trick is to clean and normalise before hashing, which turns many near duplicates into exact ones.

88
00:07:09,095 --> 00:07:11,307
That's why cleaning comes first.

89
00:07:12,157 --> 00:07:12,836
Part five.

90
00:07:12,836 --> 00:07:14,397
Splits without leakage.

91
00:07:15,247 --> 00:07:19,999
Splits measure how well the model generalises to text it hasn't seen.

92
00:07:19,999 --> 00:07:28,745
The rules: split by whole document, not by paragraph; eighty, ten, ten is typical; and set a seed so the split is reproducible.

93
00:07:28,745 --> 00:07:33,910
The function copies the list, shuffles it with a fixed seed, and slices it.

94
00:07:33,910 --> 00:07:48,097
Leakage example: if chapter one of a book is in training and chapter two is in validation, the model already knows the author's style and characters, and the validation score looks better than it really is.

95
00:07:48,097 --> 00:07:50,163
Keep whole documents together.

96
00:07:51,013 --> 00:07:54,497
And here's a real bug in the book's worked example.

97
00:07:54,497 --> 00:08:03,512
It splits three documents with train at point three four and validation at point three three, and claims one document in each split.

98
00:08:03,512 --> 00:08:09,112
But int of three times point three three is int of point nine nine, which is zero.

99
00:08:09,112 --> 00:08:12,664
So validation is empty, and test gets two documents.

100
00:08:12,664 --> 00:08:18,264
The book's own notebook prints exactly that: train one, validation zero, test two.

101
00:08:18,264 --> 00:08:25,982
The same example also says the bird flew over the house has length thirty three, but it's twenty nine characters.

102
00:08:25,982 --> 00:08:34,314
The lesson: int truncates, so for tiny data sets compute split sizes with round, or guarantee at least one item per split.

103
00:08:35,164 --> 00:08:35,792
Part six.

104
00:08:35,792 --> 00:08:38,023
Chunking for the context window.

105
00:08:38,873 --> 00:08:43,753
Transformers have a fixed context window, so long text must be cut into chunks.

106
00:08:43,753 --> 00:08:55,922
The function takes windows of eight hundred characters, and advances by eight hundred minus the overlap of two hundred, a stride of six hundred, so neighbouring chunks share two hundred characters.

107
00:08:55,922 --> 00:09:07,289
The rule of thumb is about four characters per token for English, so eight hundred characters is roughly two hundred tokens, leaving room inside a five hundred and twelve token window.

108
00:09:08,139 --> 00:09:10,116
Here's the overlap visualised.

109
00:09:10,116 --> 00:09:18,156
Chunk one is zero to eight hundred, chunk two six hundred to fourteen hundred, chunk three twelve hundred to two thousand.

110
00:09:18,156 --> 00:09:22,506
A sentence near position seven hundred appears whole in chunk two.

111
00:09:22,506 --> 00:09:34,632
One side effect the book doesn't mention: with two thousand characters, the loop makes a fourth chunk from eighteen hundred to two thousand, which is entirely contained in chunk three.

112
00:09:34,632 --> 00:09:36,280
That's a small duplicate.

113
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:40,431
You can stop the loop once a chunk reaches the end of the text.

114
00:09:41,281 --> 00:09:48,895
Store the result as JSON lines, with fields for the text, its source, its split, and optionally its length.

115
00:09:48,895 --> 00:09:58,927
Then run quality checks on every split: counts, average and maximum lengths, the most common words, and a few random samples read by a human.

116
00:09:58,927 --> 00:10:13,727
Red flags include HTML words like div or href among the top words, a duplicate rate above five percent, a maximum length many times the average, and the replacement character that signals an encoding problem.

117
00:10:13,727 --> 00:10:20,699
And always deduplicate before splitting, otherwise the same chunk can land in both train and test.

118
00:10:21,549 --> 00:10:27,024
Here's the notebook's complete walkthrough, as it actually printed on my run.

119
00:10:27,024 --> 00:10:31,788
Four raw documents, two of them identical once the HTML is removed.

120
00:10:31,788 --> 00:10:35,271
Cleaning normalises the tab and the extra spaces.

121
00:10:35,271 --> 00:10:37,191
Deduplication leaves three.

122
00:10:37,191 --> 00:10:40,746
The split gives one, zero and two, as we just saw.

123
00:10:40,746 --> 00:10:47,571
The single training document becomes one chunk, and the JSON record has a length of twenty four.

124
00:10:47,571 --> 00:10:54,823
The book's printed version shows a different, impossible split, so always trust the run over the page.

125
00:10:55,673 --> 00:11:04,375
The notebook includes a sanity check function that prints the count, average, maximum and minimum length of the chunks, plus a sample.

126
00:11:04,375 --> 00:11:14,051
On the toy corpus it found four chunks, average sixty three characters, no HTML leakage, and it warned that one chunk is shorter than ten characters.

127
00:11:14,051 --> 00:11:19,117
That's exactly the kind of thing you want to catch before training, not after.

128
00:11:19,117 --> 00:11:20,740
Run it after every stage.

129
00:11:21,590 --> 00:11:22,969
Two more quick checks.

130
00:11:22,969 --> 00:11:26,979
The duplicate rate is one minus unique hashes over total hashes.

131
00:11:26,979 --> 00:11:30,739
And top words just counts the most frequent lowercase words.

132
00:11:30,739 --> 00:11:34,624
If markup words like div or href show up, cleaning isn't done.

133
00:11:34,624 --> 00:11:41,016
On the Time Machine, the top words were the, and, then I, at fifteen hundred and thirty eight, and of.

134
00:11:41,016 --> 00:11:45,966
The capital I ranks third because the story is told by a first person narrator.

135
00:11:45,966 --> 00:11:49,601
Top words tell you about your corpus, not just about bugs.

136
00:11:50,451 --> 00:11:55,369
Let's stress the ordering rule, because it's the most common pipeline mistake.

137
00:11:55,369 --> 00:12:03,943
If you split first and deduplicate afterwards within each split, a paragraph that appears twice can land once in train and once in test.

138
00:12:03,943 --> 00:12:08,672
Then the model has literally seen the test answer, and the test score lies.

139
00:12:08,672 --> 00:12:14,094
On web scale data, boilerplate repeats thousands of times, so this matters enormously.

140
00:12:14,094 --> 00:12:17,309
The order is clean, deduplicate, split, then chunk.

141
00:12:18,159 --> 00:12:29,660
And here's what a provenance README might look like: each file, its author, its source with the Gutenberg number, its licence status, and a one line summary of how it was processed.

142
00:12:29,660 --> 00:12:35,759
It takes two minutes to write, and future you, or anyone reviewing your model, will be grateful.

143
00:12:36,609 --> 00:12:37,410
Part seven.

144
00:12:37,410 --> 00:12:39,084
A real book experiment.

145
00:12:39,934 --> 00:12:43,534
I ran three public domain novels through the same steps.

146
00:12:43,534 --> 00:12:50,221
First I stripped Project Gutenberg's licence header and footer, which is about ten percent of each file.

147
00:12:50,221 --> 00:12:53,692
Pure boilerplate you don't want the model to memorise.

148
00:12:53,692 --> 00:12:58,771
Then I split into paragraphs, cleaned them, deduplicated, filtered and chunked.

149
00:12:58,771 --> 00:13:07,965
The duplicate rate was only zero point three eight percent: things like the one word line Well, in quotes, which appears seven times, and by H.

150
00:13:07,965 --> 00:13:08,094
G.

151
00:13:08,094 --> 00:13:09,251
Wells three times.

152
00:13:09,251 --> 00:13:16,516
But three hundred and thirty eight paragraphs were shorter than forty characters, mostly short lines of dialogue.

153
00:13:16,516 --> 00:13:22,495
The book's length filter drops all of them, which for fiction may be throwing away good data.

154
00:13:22,495 --> 00:13:25,774
Filters are policy decisions, not universal truths.

155
00:13:26,623 --> 00:13:29,669
Is the four characters per token rule right?

156
00:13:29,669 --> 00:13:33,198
I ran GPT-2's real tokenizer over The Time Machine.

157
00:13:33,198 --> 00:13:39,704
The answer is four point three characters per token, so the rule holds well for English prose.

158
00:13:39,704 --> 00:13:54,238
The eight hundred character chunks came out between a hundred and eight and two hundred and forty six tokens, averaging a hundred and eighty seven, all comfortably inside a five hundred and twelve token window.

159
00:13:54,238 --> 00:13:59,428
Victorian prose with long words and dialogue gives a fair amount of spread.

160
00:14:00,278 --> 00:14:00,982
Part eight.

161
00:14:00,982 --> 00:14:01,750
Wrapping up.

162
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:04,516
My answers to the exercises.

163
00:14:04,516 --> 00:14:11,496
Hash detective: a duplicated paragraph gives an identical SHA-1, and with a set each check is instant.

164
00:14:11,496 --> 00:14:24,156
Splitter check with seed one twenty three and twelve documents: int of nine point six is nine for training, int of one point two is one for validation, and the remaining two go to test.

165
00:14:24,156 --> 00:14:26,688
A rerun gives exactly the same split.

166
00:14:26,688 --> 00:14:42,564
Chunk reality, three thousand characters with eight hundred and two hundred: chunks start at zero, six hundred, twelve hundred, eighteen hundred and twenty four hundred, so five chunks, with the last one six hundred characters long.

167
00:14:42,564 --> 00:14:51,597
And the end of chapter exercises, the full pipeline, the statistics and the casing comparison, are what I did with the Wells novels.

168
00:14:52,447 --> 00:14:53,654
The key takeaways.

169
00:14:53,654 --> 00:14:57,409
Data quality is the cheapest accuracy boost you can buy.

170
00:14:57,409 --> 00:15:01,566
Use text you're allowed to use, and record where it came from.

171
00:15:01,566 --> 00:15:06,193
Clean it, deduplicate before splitting, and split by whole documents.

172
00:15:06,193 --> 00:15:12,899
Chunk with overlap, remembering about four characters per token, four point three in my measurement.

173
00:15:12,899 --> 00:15:17,258
And store it as JSON lines, with sanity checks after every stage.

174
00:15:18,107 --> 00:15:20,233
My notebook run and book check.

175
00:15:20,233 --> 00:15:26,060
The chapter seven notebook ran end to end on a CPU, all eleven code cells, no errors.

176
00:15:26,060 --> 00:15:34,355
Three errata: the worked example's split is one, zero, two rather than one, one, one, which the notebook itself confirms.

177
00:15:34,355 --> 00:15:38,606
The bird sentence is twenty nine characters, not thirty three.

178
00:15:38,606 --> 00:15:41,622
And the SHA-1 collision odds are overstated.

179
00:15:41,622 --> 00:15:46,353
Plus one note: the chunking loop can produce a redundant final chunk.

180
00:15:47,203 --> 00:15:49,849
The review questions, with my answers.

181
00:15:49,849 --> 00:15:56,116
One: lowercasing shrinks the vocabulary and merges variants, but loses names and acronyms.

182
00:15:56,116 --> 00:16:01,408
Keep case when they matter, or when you want natural looking generated text.

183
00:16:01,408 --> 00:16:09,764
Two: deduplication prevents memorisation and wasted compute, and stops inflated evaluation when duplicates cross splits.

184
00:16:09,764 --> 00:16:19,303
Three: a hash is a fixed length fingerprint, and SHA-1 is fine for accidental duplicates at small scale, though not against an adversary.

185
00:16:19,303 --> 00:16:26,823
Four: split by document because text from the same document is correlated, which would leak into validation.

186
00:16:26,823 --> 00:16:32,324
Five: overlap ensures text near a boundary appears whole in at least one chunk.

187
00:16:32,324 --> 00:16:38,034
Six: JSON lines is streamable, greppable and appendable, with one record per line.

188
00:16:38,884 --> 00:16:40,106
Your practice list.

189
00:16:40,106 --> 00:16:46,984
Run the chapter seven notebook, and fix the worked example's split so every set gets at least one document.

190
00:16:46,984 --> 00:16:52,255
Download a Gutenberg book, strip its header and footer, and run the full pipeline.

191
00:16:52,255 --> 00:16:55,212
Measure characters per token on your own text.

192
00:16:55,212 --> 00:16:58,876
Compare the top fifty words with and without lowercasing.

193
00:16:58,876 --> 00:17:03,119
And keep your train dot jsonl, because chapter eight tokenizes it.

194
00:17:03,969 --> 00:17:05,435
That's chapter seven.

195
00:17:05,435 --> 00:17:09,346
We now have clean, deduplicated, well split text chunks.

196
00:17:09,346 --> 00:17:19,051
In chapter eight, we turn those strings into token IDs, building byte pair encoding from scratch and comparing it to production tokenizers.

197
